    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for clarifying. but i'm still confused about the mechanics of why that happens. if the p-value says it's significant, why would the confidence interval suggest it might not be? i guess i'm struggling with the concept of \"random error\" or noise in the data making this discrepancy happen. can you explain how the error term affects both?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The discrepancy between statistical significance and clinical importance arises from the nature of random error or noise in the data. In a hypothesis test, the p-value measures the probability of observing an effect as extreme or more extreme than the one observed, assuming that the true effect is exactly 0. Random error or noise in the data can inflate the standard error of the estimate, reducing the precision of the estimate. As a result, even a statistically significant effect may appear small within a wide confidence interval, indicating that the effect is not practically significant due to the level of noise in the data.",
      "source": "target",
